<h1>About</h1>

<details class="about-section" data-section="me">
  <summary><h2>Me</h2></summary>
  <p>
      I am a front-end engineer who builds applications and components using
      JavaScript, HTML, and CSS. I love building cool things, especially using
      JavaScript. I have worked on small and large code bases and enjoy the
      unique challenges presented by both — today as a Senior Staff Frontend
      Engineer at Pendo.io. See my full <a href="/resume/">Resume</a>.
  </p>

  {# Strava Widget (see CONTEXT.md) — placement/visual design here are
     deliberately minimal, not polished: guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#24's
     map explicitly deferred widget visual design/placement as a follow-on
     effort once the data contract existed; this just gets it on the page.
     Guarded with {% if strava %} since src/_data/strava.json doesn't exist
     until the Refresh Job's first run completes — an Eleventy global data
     file is optional, so referencing an absent one is safe, not a build
     error. #}
  {% if strava %}
  <section class="strava-widget" aria-labelledby="running-heading">
      <h3 id="running-heading">Running</h3>
      <p>
          I enjoy running and try to do so as often as possible. I prefer trail
          running, but mostly train on paved roads for time/convenience reasons.
          Below you can find data about my most recent runs:
      </p>
      <h4>Recent Activities</h4>
      {# A compact table, not a prose list — six columns of genuinely
         tabular data (guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#70), same reasoning
         as the Lighthouse widget's table (guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#41).
         Only the table scrolls sideways on a narrow screen, same pattern as
         the Lighthouse widget (guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#124) — a full
         stacked-card reflow wasn't judged worth it for a single-line-per-row
         table this narrow. role + aria-label give the scrolling region an
         accessible name — WCAG 1.4.10 (Reflow) exempts genuinely two-
         dimensional data like a table from its no-horizontal-scrolling
         rule, but only once the scroll mechanism itself is operable (WCAG
         2.1.1). The .scroll-region marker gets its tabindex from
         /js/scroll-region.js, not a static attribute here — this section
         starts collapsed and the table often fits without overflowing even
         once open, so a tabindex="0" that's always present would be a dead
         tab stop most of the time. #}
      <div
          class="strava-activities-scroll scroll-region"
          role="region"
          aria-label="Recent Activities"
      >
          <table>
              <thead>
                  <tr>
                      <th scope="col">Activity Type</th>
                      <th scope="col">Activity Name</th>
                      <th scope="col">Distance</th>
                      <th scope="col">Pace</th>
                      <th scope="col">Elevation</th>
                      <th scope="col">Date</th>
                  </tr>
              </thead>
              <tbody>
                  {% for activity in strava.activities %}
                  <tr>
                      <td>{{ activity.type | activityTypeLabel(activity.sport_type) }}</td>
                      <td>{{ activity.name }}</td>
                      <td>{{ activity.distance | metersToMiles | round(1) }} mi</td>
                      <td>{{ activity.distance | paceMinPerMile(activity.moving_time) }} /mi</td>
                      <td>{{ (activity.elevation_gain or 0) | metersToFeet | round }} ft</td>
                      <td><time datetime="{{ activity.start_date }}">{{ activity.start_date | readableDate }}</time></td>
                  </tr>
                  {% endfor %}
              </tbody>
          </table>
      </div>
    <h4>
        Recent Stats <span class="strava-stats-window">(last 4 weeks)</span>
    </h4>
    <ul class="strava-stats">
        {% for sport, totals in strava.stats %}
        <li>
            <strong>{{ sport }}</strong>:
            {{ totals.count }} {{ "activity" if totals.count == 1 else "activities" }},
            {{ totals.distance | metersToMiles | round(1) }} mi
        </li>
      {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    {% if strava.runBreakdown | length %}
    {# Run Breakdown (see CONTEXT.md): Road Run vs. Trail Run average
       mileage/pace, computed by the Refresh Job from Activities directly
       since Strava's own Stats endpoint has no Trail Run/Run split. `|
       length` rather than a bare truthiness check — buildRunBreakdown
       returns `{}` (truthy) during a running-free window, and a bare
       {% if strava.runBreakdown %} would render this heading with an
       empty list beneath it. #}
    <h4>
        Run Breakdown <span class="strava-stats-window">(last 4 weeks)</span>
    </h4>
    <ul class="strava-run-breakdown">
        {% for label, totals in strava.runBreakdown %}
        <li>
            <strong>{{ label }}</strong>:
            {{ (totals.distance / totals.count) | metersToMiles | round(1) }} mi avg,
            {{ totals.distance | paceMinPerMile(totals.moving_time) }} /mi avg
        </li>
        {% endfor %}
    </ul>
    {% endif %}
        <p class="strava-updated">
            Last updated {{ strava.capturedAt | readableDate }}
        </p>
    </section>
  {% else %}
    <p class="strava-widget-pending">
        Strava activity data will appear here once the Refresh Job's first run
        completes.
    </p>
  {% endif %}
</details>

<details class="about-section" data-section="site">
    <summary>
        <h2>
            This site
        </h2>
    </summary>
    <h3>
        Performance
    </h3>

  {# Latest run plus the one before it (may not exist yet — e.g. right
     after guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#71's Snapshot-shape migration, or
     before the second-ever audit) for the trend indicators below. See
     ADR 0006 for why the Snapshot retains history at all. #}
  {% set latest = lighthouse.history[0] %}
  {% set previous = lighthouse.history[1] %}
  {% set previousDate = (previous.capturedAt | readableDate) if previous else none %}
  {% set metricNames = {
    "performance": "Performance", "accessibility": "Accessibility",
    "best-practices": "Best Practices", "seo": "SEO",
    "lcp": "LCP", "cls": "CLS", "tbt": "TBT"
  } %}
  {# Renders one metric value the way its column wants it — LCP in seconds,
     TBT in whole ms, everything else (0-100 scores, unitless CLS) bare.
     One macro so the cell text and the trend toggletip's copy can't drift. #}
  {% macro fmtMetric(key, value) -%}
    {%- if key == "lcp" -%}{{ (value / 1000) | round(1) }}s
    {%- elif key == "tbt" -%}{{ value | round }}ms
    {%- else -%}{{ value }}
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endmacro -%}
  {# Trend marker for one cell. An actual tier change (improved/regressed)
     is a native-Popover toggletip button (see ADR 0007); a flat arrow is
     plain text; no previous run means no marker at all. Builds its own
     popover id and aria-label so the two column loops below don't repeat
     that derivation. #}
  {% macro trendMarker(change, pageLabel, metricName, metricKey, detail) -%}
    {%- if change and change.outcome != "flat" -%}
      {%- set detailId = "lh-trend-" ~ (pageLabel | lower | replace(" ", "-")) ~ "-" ~ metricKey -%}
      <button type="button" class="lh-trend lh-trend-{{ change.outcome }}" popovertarget="{{ detailId }}" aria-label="{{ metricName }} {{ change.outcome }} since previous audit" aria-describedby="{{ detailId }}">{{ "↑" if change.direction == "up" else "↓" }}</button><span popover id="{{ detailId }}" class="lh-trend-detail">{{ detail }}</span>
    {%- elif change -%}
      <span class="lh-trend lh-trend-flat" aria-label="No tier change since previous audit">–</span>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- endmacro -%}
  {# The ⓘ toggletip trigger for a metric column's definition (ADR 0008).
     `place` is "head" or "cell": the header copy shows on desktop, the cell
     copy in the mobile card layout, display-swapped at 640px so exactly one
     is ever active. Both point at the same <span popover> by id, which also
     stays the target of each <th>'s aria-describedby. Full metric names live
     here only — the headers themselves show just the short form, and each
     <th> is aria-labelledby its short label so this button's name doesn't
     get folded into the column header announced on every data cell. No
     title: it would be a hover-only tooltip, the pattern ADR 0008 rejects. #}
  {% macro defTrigger(key, place) -%}
    {%- set fullNames = {
      "performance": "Performance", "accessibility": "Accessibility",
      "best-practices": "Best Practices", "seo": "SEO",
      "lcp": "Largest Contentful Paint", "cls": "Cumulative Layout Shift",
      "tbt": "Total Blocking Time"
    } -%}
    <button type="button" class="lh-def-trigger lh-def-trigger-{{ place }}" popovertarget="lh-def-{{ key }}" aria-label="{{ fullNames[key] }} — what this column measures" aria-describedby="lh-def-{{ key }}"><span aria-hidden="true">ⓘ</span></button>
  {%- endmacro -%}
  <div class="lighthouse-widget">
    {# Only the table scrolls sideways on a narrow screen (see #41); the
       legend and definition panels below stay outside the scroll wrapper so
       a horizontal scrollbar can't drag a vertical one in with it (#124).
       role + aria-labelledby (pointing at the table's own <caption> rather
       than a separate static label) give the scrolling region an
       accessible name — WCAG 1.4.10 (Reflow) exempts genuinely two-
       dimensional data like a table from its no-horizontal-scrolling rule,
       but only once the scroll mechanism itself is operable (WCAG 2.1.1,
       #184). The .scroll-region marker gets its tabindex from
       /js/scroll-region.js, not a static attribute here — this table
       reflows to non-scrolling stacked cards below 640px (see the media
       query below), and a tabindex="0" that's always present would be a
       dead tab stop there once nothing overflows to scroll to. #}
    <div
        class="lighthouse-table-scroll scroll-region"
        role="region"
        aria-labelledby="lh-table-caption"
    >
      <table>
        <caption id="lh-table-caption">
          Lighthouse scores, last measured {{ latest.capturedAt | readableDate }}
          {%- if previous %}
          — trend vs. previous audit ({{ previousDate }})
          {%- endif %}
        </caption>
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th scope="col">Page</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Performance" aria-labelledby="lh-col-performance" aria-describedby="lh-def-performance"><span id="lh-col-performance">Perf</span>{{ defTrigger("performance", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Accessibility" aria-labelledby="lh-col-accessibility" aria-describedby="lh-def-accessibility"><span id="lh-col-accessibility">A11y</span>{{ defTrigger("accessibility", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Best Practices" aria-labelledby="lh-col-best-practices" aria-describedby="lh-def-best-practices"><span id="lh-col-best-practices">BP</span>{{ defTrigger("best-practices", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" aria-labelledby="lh-col-seo" aria-describedby="lh-def-seo"><span id="lh-col-seo">SEO</span>{{ defTrigger("seo", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Largest Contentful Paint" aria-labelledby="lh-col-lcp" aria-describedby="lh-def-lcp"><span id="lh-col-lcp">LCP</span>{{ defTrigger("lcp", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Cumulative Layout Shift" aria-labelledby="lh-col-cls" aria-describedby="lh-def-cls"><span id="lh-col-cls">CLS</span>{{ defTrigger("cls", "head") }}</th>
            <th scope="col" abbr="Total Blocking Time" aria-labelledby="lh-col-tbt" aria-describedby="lh-def-tbt"><span id="lh-col-tbt">TBT</span>{{ defTrigger("tbt", "head") }}</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          {% for label, page in latest.pages %}
          {% set prevPage = previous.pages[label] if previous else none %}
          <tr>
            <th scope="row">{{ label }}</th>
            {% for key in ["performance", "accessibility", "best-practices", "seo"] %}
            {% set value = page.scores[key] %}
            {% set prevValue = prevPage.scores[key] if prevPage else none %}
            {% set level = value | scoreLevel %}
            {% set prevLevel = (prevValue | scoreLevel) if prevPage else none %}
            {% set change = level | trend(prevLevel) %}
            {% set detail = {
              label: metricNames[key], currentDisplay: value, currentLevel: level,
              previousDisplay: prevValue, previousLevel: prevLevel, previousDate: previousDate
            } | trendDetail %}
            {# Score Donut on every category cell; no Trend Sparkline on any
               of them — sparklineSvg() has no fixed domain for a category
               score and returns "" (Performance included, deliberately: its
               donut already carries the current value, so a sparkline read
               as redundant — guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#158). The donut
               is aria-hidden inline SVG; the numeral stays real text and the
               tier arrow keeps carrying the trend
               (guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#151, ADR 0009). #}
            {% set spark = lighthouse.history | historyValues(label, key) | sparklineSvg(key) %}
            <td class="lh-cell lh-{{ level }}" data-label="{{ metricNames[key] }}"><span class="lh-cell-inner"><span class="lh-cell-row"><span class="lh-donut-wrap">{{ value | donutSvg(level) | safe }}<span class="lh-donut-num">{{ value }}</span></span>{{ trendMarker(change, label, metricNames[key], key, detail) }}{{ defTrigger(key, "cell") }}</span>{{ spark | safe }}</span></td>
            {% endfor %}
            {% for key in ["lcp", "cls", "tbt"] %}
            {% set value = page[key] %}
            {% set prevValue = prevPage[key] if prevPage else none %}
            {% set level = value | metricLevel(key) %}
            {% set prevLevel = (prevValue | metricLevel(key)) if prevPage else none %}
            {% set change = level | trend(prevLevel) %}
            {% set detail = {
              label: metricNames[key], currentDisplay: fmtMetric(key, value) | trim, currentLevel: level,
              previousDisplay: (fmtMetric(key, prevValue) | trim) if prevValue is not none else none,
              previousLevel: prevLevel, previousDate: previousDate
            } | trendDetail %}
            {# Trend Sparkline on LCP/CLS/TBT (guntherjh/guntherjh.github.io#151,
               ADR 0009). No donut — these have no natural 0–100 max; the
               threshold-coloured numeral stays as-is. #}
            {% set spark = lighthouse.history | historyValues(label, key) | sparklineSvg(key) %}
            <td class="lh-cell lh-{{ level }}" data-label="{{ metricNames[key] }}"><span class="lh-cell-inner"><span class="lh-cell-row">{{ fmtMetric(key, value) }}{{ trendMarker(change, label, metricNames[key], key, detail) }}{{ defTrigger(key, "cell") }}</span>{{ spark | safe }}</span></td>
            {% endfor %}
          </tr>
          {% endfor %}
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <ul class="lighthouse-legend">
      <li>Green = good, orange = needs improvement, red = poor, per Lighthouse's own thresholds.</li>
      <li>The faint line in a cell traces that metric across the last few audits (newest at right); it plots raw values, not tiers, so short wobbles are normal lab noise — the ↑ ↓ – arrow is the real tier signal.</li>
      <li>↑ ↓ – show whether a score's tier improved, regressed, or held since the previous audit — activate ↑ or ↓ for the earlier value.</li>
      <li>Select ⓘ next to a metric for more about it.</li>
    </ul>
    {# One definition panel per metric column (ADR 0008). Each is the target
       of its column header's and cells' ⓘ toggletip triggers and of every
       matching <th>'s aria-describedby, so the text still reaches assistive
       tech when the Popover API is unsupported and the button is inert. #}
    <span popover id="lh-def-performance" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Performance (Perf)</strong> — Lighthouse's weighted score (0–100) combining lab metrics — weighted most heavily toward LCP, TBT and CLS. ≥ 90 good, 50–89 needs improvement, &lt; 50 poor.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-accessibility" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Accessibility (A11y)</strong> — Weighted pass/fail automated accessibility checks (Lighthouse runs axe-core). Same 0–100 banding. A perfect score still needs manual testing to confirm — automated checks can't catch everything.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-best-practices" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Best Practices (BP)</strong> — Equally-weighted general web-quality and security checks — HTTPS, no console errors, no deprecated APIs, correct image aspect ratios, a Content Security Policy. Same 0–100 banding.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-seo" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>SEO</strong> — Basic technical-SEO checks — page is crawlable, has a title and meta description, returns a successful status, uses descriptive link text and a valid canonical. Fundamentals only, not a full SEO audit. Same 0–100 banding.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-lcp" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Largest Contentful Paint (LCP)</strong> — Time until the largest image or text block in the viewport renders. ≤ 2.5 s good, 2.5–4 s needs improvement, &gt; 4 s poor.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-cls" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Cumulative Layout Shift (CLS)</strong> — How much visible content shifts position unexpectedly while the page loads (unitless). ≤ 0.1 good, 0.1–0.25 needs improvement, &gt; 0.25 poor.</span>
    <span popover id="lh-def-tbt" class="lh-def-detail"><strong>Total Blocking Time (TBT)</strong> — Total time the main thread was blocked by long tasks during load, delaying response to input. ≤ 200 ms good, 200–600 ms needs improvement, &gt; 600 ms poor.</span>
  </div>
</details>

<script src="/js/about-sections.js" defer></script>
<script src="/js/scroll-region.js" defer></script>
